Why should we avoid using array index as a key in some situations?
{vendors.map((vendor, index) => (
    <div key={index}>
        {vendor.name}
    </div>
))}

It can work if the list is static and never changes order.

But imagine:

Before:

Index 0 → Vendor A
Index 1 → Vendor B
Index 2 → Vendor C

Now Vendor A is removed:

After:

Index 0 → Vendor B
Index 1 → Vendor C

The indexes changed.

Before              After

0 → A               0 → B
1 → B               1 → C
2 → C

We should avoid using the array index as a key when the list can be reordered, inserted into, or deleted from. The index can change when the list changes, so it doesn't provide a stable identity. If possible, we should use a unique and stable ID from the data


